        "https://github.com/homeassistant-ai/ha-mcp/releases/tag/v9.1.2"
      ],
      "description": "HA-MCP is ==an MCP server for configuring and automating Home Assistant== from Claude, ChatGPT and other AI assistants. Its recommended install is an integration that runs the server inside Home Assistant itself; an add-on, Docker, PyPI and local stdio are alternatives.",
      "why_interesting": "First time on our list. Version 9.0.0 (28 October) turned the in-process integration into a full install with ==locked-down defaults, an access on/off switch and allowlisted OAuth callbacks==, and the README says Home Assistant 2026.11 lists it in the built-in Marketplace, so no custom repository is needed. v9.1.2 followed on 8 November.",
      "caveat": "The server ==acts as a Home Assistant administrator==, and the README itself warns that a long-lived token works from anywhere if it leaks; it suggests a dedicated account instead. An AI client can then change automations and real devices, the in-process server needs Home Assistant 2026.8 or newer, and dev builds ship several times a day. We did not install it."
    },
    {
      "repository": "garrytan/gbrain",
      "url": "https://github.com/garrytan/gbrain",
      "trending_rank": 6,
      "category": "AI agents / memory",
      "language": "TypeScript",
      "license": "MIT",
      "stars_total": 32309,
      "stars_period": 216,
      "stars_period_label": "216 stars today",
      "archived": false,
      "metadata_checked_at": "2026-11-10T07:02:00Z",
      "readme_checked_at": "2026-11-10T07:01:27Z",
      "readme_source_url": "https://api.github.com/repos/garrytan/gbrain/readme",
      "evidence_urls": [
        "https://github.com/garrytan/gbrain",
        "https://api.github.com/repos/garrytan/gbrain",
        "https://github.com/garrytan/gbrain/releases/tag/v0.60.640.0"
      ],
      "description": "GBrain is ==a self-hostable memory layer for AI agents==, written by Y Combinator CEO Garry Tan. It stores facts with their sources, supports corrections and withdrawals, and shares that memory across agents over MCP, starting with keyless keyword search and adding semantic search, cited synthesis and a typed knowledge graph.",
      "why_interesting": "First time on our list. Most agent memory tools return chunks; GBrain's README promises ==a cited answer plus a note on what the brain does not know==. It runs on embedded PGlite with no server or model key to start, and the author says it powers his own agents across about 155,000 pages, a claim we did not verify.",
      "caveat": "It ==changes very fast==: 20 tagged releases in the 24 hours before our check, and it needs Bun 1.4.4 or newer. The README notes that configured cloud embedding and synthesis providers receive your text and that Markdown export is not a full backup. The quick install pipes remote scripts into a shell, and gbrain.io is a paid hosted option. We did not install it."
    },
    {
      "repository": "vercel-labs/agent-browser",
